Kind: captions
Language: en

NOTE
VoteChain contributor walkthrough captions
Timings based on the transcript in docs/video-walkthrough.md.
Update timestamps once the actual recording is available.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Welcome to VoteChain! In this walkthrough I'm going to show you how to go
from zero to your first contribution in about ten minutes.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:15.000
We'll clone the repo, install the dependencies, run the full test suite,
make a small change, and run the tests one more time to confirm nothing broke.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Let's get started.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
First, open a terminal. We'll clone the VoteChain repository from GitHub.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Now let me give you a quick tour of the project layout. At the top level you'll see
a contracts/ directory — that holds the two Soroban smart contracts:
governance and token.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:05.000
There's a docs/ directory with Architecture Decision Records and guides, a scripts/
directory for deployment helpers, and a Makefile that wraps the most common commands.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Almost everything you'll do during development goes through make.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Let's install the tools we need. If you already have Rust installed you can
skip the first step.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:55.000
Run the rustup installer from rustup.rs, follow the on-screen prompts,
then restart your terminal and verify with: rustc --version

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Next, add the WebAssembly target with: rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown
Soroban contracts compile to WebAssembly, so Cargo needs this target to build them.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Install the Stellar CLI at the pinned version: cargo install --locked stellar-cli@22.8.2
We pin the CLI to a specific version to keep everyone's environment consistent.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:45.000
The required version is always documented in the Makefile and README.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Now let's run the tests to confirm the environment is set up correctly.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:18.000
Run: make test
Cargo compiles both contracts and runs all unit tests.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:35.000
On the first run this can take a minute or two because it downloads and compiles
dependencies. Subsequent runs are much faster thanks to the build cache.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:50.000
When everything passes you'll see: test result: ok. 45 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored
Forty-five tests, zero failures — we're good.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:05:05.000
If you see any failures here on a fresh clone, please open a GitHub issue
so we can investigate.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Now let's make a small, safe change to prove the workflow end-to-end.
I'll add a documentation comment to one of the public functions in the governance contract.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:06:00.000
Open contracts/governance/src/lib.rs in your editor and find the proposal_count function.
We'll add a doc-comment above it.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:20.000
Now let's run the formatter and linter to make sure the change is clean.
Run: make fmt  then: make lint

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:40.000
No warnings, no errors. Let's also generate the docs to see the comment rendered
with: cargo doc --no-deps --open

00:06:40.000 --> 00:07:00.000
You can see the new doc-comment appears in the rendered HTML. This is the
kind of small, incremental improvement that makes a great first contribution.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:10.000
Always run the full test suite after any change, no matter how small.
Run: make test

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:25.000
All forty-five tests still pass. The doc-comment didn't break anything —
which is exactly what we expected, but it's important to verify.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:15.000
Let's commit the change using the Conventional Commits format.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:35.000
git add contracts/governance/src/lib.rs
git commit -m "docs(governance): add doc-comment to proposal_count"
git push origin feature/my-first-change

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:45.000
Then go to GitHub, open a pull request against main, fill in the PR template,
and reference any related issue.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:52.000
A maintainer will review it and merge it once CI passes.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:10:00.000
That's the full workflow — clone, install, test, change, test again, commit, and PR.
For more detail see the Getting Started guide and CONTRIBUTING.md. Happy coding!
